1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,140
And now for you, Scoutmaster.

2
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:14,360
When a Scout sees a tag on a tent and it says waterproof tent, that's a challenge.

3
00:00:15,260 --> 00:00:17,440
They need to find a way to pitch it so that it gets wet.

4
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:19,680
Yeah.

5
00:00:20,620 --> 00:00:26,480
When he sees a label on a frying pan that says non-stick frying pan,

6
00:00:26,760 --> 00:00:29,180
he has to find something that's going to stick to it.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,260
Last but not least, don't bring anything camping that has the label fireproof on it.

8
00:00:36,860 --> 00:00:38,660
It's just not a good idea. Trust me.

9
00:00:39,760 --> 00:00:41,700
Hey, this is podcast number 26.

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:14,020
Welcome back to the Scoutmaster's podcast.

11
00:01:14,320 --> 00:01:15,260
This is Clark Green.

12
00:01:15,540 --> 00:01:18,039
This time around in podcast number 26,

13
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:24,000
we're going to be talking about something I got an email on this week.

14
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:27,360
And I sure do appreciate those emails, folks. Keep them coming.

15
00:01:28,420 --> 00:01:32,160
But it's about cooking and menu planning and things like that.

16
00:01:32,460 --> 00:01:40,039
We're going to begin another series of thrilling, interesting, and inspiring talks about

17
00:01:40,039 --> 00:01:43,420
planning the future of your Scout unit.

18
00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:49,360
Yeah. What is Scouting going to look like in your community in the next several years?

19
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:50,920
What's your role going to be?

20
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:52,280
That's about it this time.

21
00:01:52,680 --> 00:01:54,460
Let's go ahead and get ourselves started, shall we?

22
00:01:55,840 --> 00:01:58,460
Scout Mastership in seven minutes.

23
00:01:59,340 --> 00:02:00,300
Or less.

24
00:02:00,300 --> 00:02:10,340
Ray Britton sent me an email this week.

25
00:02:10,639 --> 00:02:12,040
He's a Scoutmaster in Tennessee.

26
00:02:13,260 --> 00:02:14,040
And he says,

27
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:16,300
First of all, I want to thank you for your wonderful podcast.

28
00:02:16,920 --> 00:02:18,340
It's a great resource for me.

29
00:02:18,400 --> 00:02:22,220
You're helping me see what I need to change and adapt about our troop.

30
00:02:22,600 --> 00:02:26,220
The first year or so, I pretty much kept the program the way it had been

31
00:02:26,220 --> 00:02:31,180
while trying to educate myself on what we needed to be doing and where we needed to be going.

32
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:32,940
As you know, it's a challenge.

33
00:02:33,980 --> 00:02:38,220
And here's what I think is an example of the challenge that we face in wanting a boy-led troop

34
00:02:38,220 --> 00:02:41,260
and being frustrated by the path they choose when given free reign.

35
00:02:42,320 --> 00:02:45,860
What is the proper guidance to give Scouts when it comes to meal planning?

36
00:02:46,680 --> 00:02:53,100
My Scouts would simply eat Pop-Tarts, cold, and soft drinks, hot, and ramen.

37
00:02:53,500 --> 00:02:55,640
Yes, some have tried it uncooked.

38
00:02:56,220 --> 00:02:59,960
And many other food options which required the absolute least effort.

39
00:03:00,820 --> 00:03:02,100
I exaggerate only slightly.

40
00:03:02,280 --> 00:03:03,940
I mean, clearly they need to learn to cook.

41
00:03:04,160 --> 00:03:06,280
Clearly they should have nutritious meals.

42
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:11,760
How do we find that balance of not dictating meals but achieving the learning goals we have for them?

43
00:03:11,900 --> 00:03:16,100
Well, Ray, first of all, thank you for your kind words about the podcast.

44
00:03:17,480 --> 00:03:21,800
And you have inspired me to add a feature called

45
00:03:21,800 --> 00:03:25,660
These Things Drive Me Crazy.

46
00:03:26,220 --> 00:03:31,400
That's right.

47
00:03:32,100 --> 00:03:36,480
There are some things that just drive me absolutely crazy.

48
00:03:37,820 --> 00:03:44,140
Scouts who are uninspired by my inspiring speeches.

49
00:03:44,140 --> 00:03:46,459
That drives me crazy.

50
00:03:49,019 --> 00:04:01,859
Another thing that drives me crazy are Scouts who forget their main gear, their clothing, their shoes, their mess kit, their very brain on camp outs.

51
00:04:01,860 --> 00:04:09,100
You know what else drives me crazy?

52
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:17,399
Parents who think that meeting times begin or end a half hour before or after they actually begin or end.

53
00:04:18,399 --> 00:04:20,060
Oh, that makes me crazy.

54
00:04:20,060 --> 00:04:33,960
Another thing that makes me crazy is answering the same question 17 times in as many minutes after the answer to that question was just announced to everyone.

55
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:36,180
That'll make you crazy.

56
00:04:36,180 --> 00:04:50,860
Of course, being asked how much farther or how much longer would drive anybody crazy.

57
00:04:50,860 --> 00:05:07,900
But Ray, like you, I am driven crazy by Scouts who in a grocery store with 10,000 items buy Pop-Tarts and ramen for every single camp out.

58
00:05:07,900 --> 00:05:37,880
Just makes me crazy.

59
00:05:37,880 --> 00:05:39,880
Just making me crazy.

60
00:05:39,880 --> 00:05:42,340
My observations have anything to do with the rest of the world.

61
00:05:42,340 --> 00:05:50,180
I don't think this leaves them until their college years and often far beyond until they find a young lady and they settle down and get married.

62
00:05:50,180 --> 00:05:53,300
And, you know, that young lady kind of straightens them out a little bit.

63
00:05:53,300 --> 00:06:05,120
Scouts seem to have cast iron stomachs and metabolisms that will run just fine on things like candle wax and shellac and straw for days or weeks at a time.

64
00:06:05,120 --> 00:06:08,220
I think that's just really the way that boys are.

65
00:06:09,180 --> 00:06:10,860
For them, preparing meals is downtime.

66
00:06:11,780 --> 00:06:12,160
Come on.

67
00:06:12,740 --> 00:06:13,520
It's inconvenient.

68
00:06:14,120 --> 00:06:16,260
They need to get it over with as quickly as possible.

69
00:06:16,480 --> 00:06:16,840
Why?

70
00:06:16,960 --> 00:06:20,660
This ramen is perfectly good cold and uncooked.

71
00:06:20,660 --> 00:06:22,140
Why cook it?

72
00:06:22,180 --> 00:06:23,800
It would be a huge waste of time.

73
00:06:27,800 --> 00:06:33,060
Scouts enjoy the autonomy of choosing their own food and fixing it as they see fit, even if it does drive us crazy.

74
00:06:33,860 --> 00:06:43,900
Some of them would eat that awful ramen stuff at every meal, whether they were camping or not, if they had their way, and consider that they were living like kings.

75
00:06:43,900 --> 00:06:49,260
They think it is ambrosia, and I wouldn't feed it to a dog.

76
00:06:49,440 --> 00:06:50,700
I can't stand the stuff.

77
00:06:51,900 --> 00:06:54,940
The other thing to consider is what they're eating at home and how they're eating it.

78
00:06:54,979 --> 00:07:00,659
I would imagine that some families may sit down together to three healthy meals a day, but they're probably exceptions to the rule.

79
00:07:00,979 --> 00:07:12,099
I've often said that the week we spend at scout camp is probably the only week of the year where a scout actually sits around a table with people and eats three meals a day.

80
00:07:12,100 --> 00:07:17,840
And that those three meals are, you know, relatively nutritious and well-balanced.

81
00:07:19,380 --> 00:07:23,180
I'll bet most of our scouts have fast food or pizza at least once a week.

82
00:07:23,280 --> 00:07:29,700
Some may have it a lot more, and I'll bet most families are lucky if they sit down to dinner more than three or four times a week.

83
00:07:30,960 --> 00:07:33,360
I mean, forget about lunch and forget about breakfast.

84
00:07:33,900 --> 00:07:40,820
If they get sat down to dinner more than three or four times a week, they probably feel like Ozzie and Harriet and feel that they've accomplished something.

85
00:07:40,820 --> 00:07:49,159
And when they do sit down to dinner, that boy who is a scout in your troop and is sitting at that table has one thing in mind.

86
00:07:49,460 --> 00:07:55,100
He wants to get dinner over with as soon as possible because there's more important things to do.

87
00:07:55,880 --> 00:08:03,900
Considering these things, it's kind of a minor miracle that they actually do anything about food at all when they're camping.

88
00:08:03,900 --> 00:08:14,219
That being said, we do have a responsibility to get them along towards an understanding of healthy eating and meal planning and things like that.

89
00:08:14,299 --> 00:08:19,280
I mean, a scout is supposed to be physically fit.

90
00:08:20,080 --> 00:08:23,179
And you're not going to be physically fit unless you're eating properly.

91
00:08:23,180 --> 00:08:34,200
There's a first class requirement that talks about planning the patrol menu for a campout that includes one breakfast and one lunch and one dinner.

92
00:08:35,020 --> 00:08:38,480
And requires cooking for at least two of the meals.

93
00:08:39,200 --> 00:08:41,600
Guys who wrote the requirement are very smart.

94
00:08:43,539 --> 00:08:44,080
Yeah.

95
00:08:44,540 --> 00:08:48,360
Opening a can and pouring it into a mess kit is not actually cooking.

96
00:08:48,360 --> 00:08:48,920
Okay.

97
00:08:49,740 --> 00:08:58,460
So, they also have to include the foods from the food pyramid and they have to demonstrate how those foods meet nutritional needs.

98
00:08:58,960 --> 00:09:00,620
Scouts have to do this to advance.

99
00:09:01,400 --> 00:09:06,660
A lot of them will probably only do it once and they'll consider it a huge imposition when they do.

100
00:09:08,320 --> 00:09:14,480
Sometimes I get a scout who's really motivated in cooking and meal planning, but I got to tell you, that's not too often.

101
00:09:14,480 --> 00:09:19,200
We've often said that we've never had a scout starve to death on a weekend camping trip.

102
00:09:19,400 --> 00:09:25,640
So, a weekend of bad food is not an immediate health or safety concern in my mind.

103
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,740
Long-term trips, that's another matter.

104
00:09:29,820 --> 00:09:34,040
They have to consider nutrition more seriously when they're planning the meals.

105
00:09:34,520 --> 00:09:39,580
Now, I encourage and cajole and try to inspire an interest in camp cooking and good food.

106
00:09:39,580 --> 00:09:45,300
And I, you know, absolutely see that scouts follow rank requirements.

107
00:09:45,540 --> 00:09:51,580
But they usually revert to the ramen, the pop tarts, the donuts, things like that.

108
00:09:51,940 --> 00:10:05,640
Now, we have instituted the understanding within our troop that you are not going to bring a bag of potato chips and a 32-ounce soda and call that lunch.

109
00:10:06,420 --> 00:10:08,220
Actually, we've outlawed soda.

110
00:10:08,220 --> 00:10:10,680
It doesn't go on camping trips with us.

111
00:10:11,220 --> 00:10:13,220
Not in a patrol menu.

112
00:10:13,580 --> 00:10:23,600
If they bother to get a loaf of bread and some lunch meat and some cheese and they put that together, to me, that's an accomplishment.

113
00:10:24,200 --> 00:10:31,640
It's better than, like I say, the bag of potato chips and, I don't know, trail mix or something like that.

114
00:10:31,740 --> 00:10:34,500
The shellac and the candle wax that I mentioned earlier.

115
00:10:34,500 --> 00:10:41,580
One way of thinking about this is thinking about it in the same context as other skills in scouting like knots.

116
00:10:42,340 --> 00:10:50,060
Some scouts, they just feel like you're beating them to death when they have to do knots and lashings and things like that.

117
00:10:50,160 --> 00:10:51,900
They just can't stand it.

118
00:10:51,900 --> 00:10:56,520
They're only going to do enough to make it through to get the rank requirement.

119
00:10:57,160 --> 00:10:59,439
And they're much more interested in other things.

120
00:11:00,020 --> 00:11:05,740
Some scouts get into the knots and the lashings and they do pioneering and they like to build stuff and it's great.

121
00:11:05,740 --> 00:11:09,940
Most of them will kind of do the things around the requirement.

122
00:11:10,580 --> 00:11:21,020
Or if they become enlightened and they know that tying a few knots and learning a little bit about rope work is going to help them when they're hanging a bear bag or something like that.

123
00:11:21,120 --> 00:11:24,180
You know, they might see some value in it.

124
00:11:24,880 --> 00:11:27,500
In the end, I try to summon a bit of serenity.

125
00:11:28,440 --> 00:11:31,800
Listen, the boys are happy to have the autonomy to choose their own menu.

126
00:11:31,800 --> 00:11:35,959
Now, you can give it some definition without being very persnickety.

127
00:11:36,339 --> 00:11:41,920
I try and point them at that first class requirement and I say this is the way that you put together a menu.

128
00:11:42,660 --> 00:11:47,459
And, you know, we refer to that part of the Scout handbook that talks about these things.

129
00:11:48,040 --> 00:11:49,839
There's the balance that you're asking about.

130
00:11:50,900 --> 00:11:55,819
It's not spoiling all their fun, but just spoiling a little bit of their fun.

131
00:11:56,900 --> 00:11:58,939
It doesn't spoil their fun, I know.

132
00:11:58,940 --> 00:12:02,960
So, hopefully that helps and hopefully that'll help the rest of you.

133
00:12:03,520 --> 00:12:08,500
And if there's something that just absolutely drives you crazy about Scouting, email me.

134
00:12:08,700 --> 00:12:09,140
Let me know.

135
00:12:09,140 --> 00:12:11,660
Give me that a little bit.

136
00:12:19,180 --> 00:12:19,819
Yeah.

137
00:12:19,820 --> 00:12:48,820
¶¶

138
00:12:49,820 --> 00:12:55,840
Where is your Scout Troop, Cub Scout Pack, Venture Crew going to be in the next five years?

139
00:12:58,220 --> 00:13:00,000
That's a really good question, isn't it?

140
00:13:01,060 --> 00:13:05,980
No doubt you're working very hard at Scouting, putting a lot of time in.

141
00:13:06,560 --> 00:13:14,220
Is your Scout unit going to continue on to serve its community when it's time for you to step back?

142
00:13:15,380 --> 00:13:18,820
That's a really interesting question, isn't it?

143
00:13:18,820 --> 00:13:25,200
And it's one that we should take very seriously as volunteer leaders.

144
00:13:26,560 --> 00:13:33,180
Scouting programs falter and sometimes disappear because they don't answer that question.

145
00:13:33,340 --> 00:13:39,360
Over the next three or four podcasts, we're going to talk about these types of questions

146
00:13:39,360 --> 00:13:44,560
and some of the strategies that we can use to answer them.

147
00:13:44,560 --> 00:13:52,719
As you know, I'm a Scoutmaster, so my familiarity is mostly with the way a Boy Scout troop runs

148
00:13:52,719 --> 00:13:56,660
and we'll be concentrating on those particular questions.

149
00:13:56,660 --> 00:14:03,079
But I'm going to try and maintain broad enough scope so that this would apply to PAX and to Venture Crews as well.

150
00:14:03,080 --> 00:14:10,380
There are four basic questions about the future of your unit and its service to your community

151
00:14:10,380 --> 00:14:15,600
that you have to keep in front of you and you have to have answers to.

152
00:14:16,420 --> 00:14:23,560
Number one, what is the minimum annual number of new members, both youth and adult,

153
00:14:23,560 --> 00:14:28,560
that's really necessary to maintain the unit?

154
00:14:28,560 --> 00:14:33,040
Number two, who are our key unit leaders for the next four or five years?

155
00:14:33,040 --> 00:14:40,560
Number three, how do we reach and or maintain financial stability?

156
00:14:40,560 --> 00:14:47,219
Number four, do we meet key indicators of effectively presenting our program?

157
00:14:47,220 --> 00:14:55,820
Now, all four of those things need to be in your mind if you're going to be a good, effective leader

158
00:14:55,820 --> 00:15:02,220
and as you prepare to pass off your responsibilities to the next person and the next group of people,

159
00:15:02,700 --> 00:15:08,800
do you have answers for them about this and can you get them to start thinking about these things

160
00:15:08,800 --> 00:15:10,920
as soon as they take on their position?

161
00:15:10,920 --> 00:15:18,240
I would guess the tenure of most adult leaders in scouting, at least in my experience,

162
00:15:18,939 --> 00:15:25,500
the tenure of most adult leaders lasts about as long as their children's participation in scouting.

163
00:15:25,860 --> 00:15:32,380
A relatively few, let's say maybe three to four out of ten,

164
00:15:33,560 --> 00:15:39,660
a relatively few will continue on for a year or two when their children basically age out of the program

165
00:15:39,660 --> 00:15:40,939
and they're not involved anymore.

166
00:15:41,079 --> 00:15:44,100
A couple of those will just stay with it for even longer.

167
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:51,400
And successive groups of leaders often have little to define the way forward when experienced leaders move on

168
00:15:51,400 --> 00:15:55,020
and they have to retrench the foundations of the program

169
00:15:55,020 --> 00:15:59,300
and when you have to do a fresh start like that, it's like starting a whole new unit

170
00:15:59,300 --> 00:16:01,719
and it can waste a lot of energy.

171
00:16:02,719 --> 00:16:06,579
You might lose membership, morale might get a little weak,

172
00:16:06,580 --> 00:16:13,700
discouragement can set in and it's a lot of effort to extend towards reaching past levels of service.

173
00:16:14,420 --> 00:16:21,300
For those units that do recover, you know, the curve may be very shallow and very long

174
00:16:21,300 --> 00:16:25,120
to get back to where they were at one time.

175
00:16:26,420 --> 00:16:31,840
Sometimes the leadership, you know, that comes in on one of these fresh starts

176
00:16:31,840 --> 00:16:36,540
has to spend so much energy to bring the unit back, they're ready to move on.

177
00:16:37,260 --> 00:16:40,980
You know, within a couple of years, they may feel kind of beaten up and discouraged

178
00:16:40,980 --> 00:16:43,220
and now the whole process is going to start all over again.

179
00:16:43,740 --> 00:16:48,780
And this is where, you know, what was once a vibrant cub pack five years ago

180
00:16:48,780 --> 00:16:51,740
now has 12 guys and it's kind of fallen to pieces.

181
00:16:51,740 --> 00:16:57,680
Or a scout troop that has a long history is down to eight or nine boys

182
00:16:57,680 --> 00:17:01,580
and kind of a disinterested, detached leadership.

183
00:17:01,800 --> 00:17:03,080
Now, some of this is natural.

184
00:17:04,020 --> 00:17:07,140
I mean, you know, there's a handful of troops that have been around

185
00:17:07,140 --> 00:17:10,980
since the founding of the Boy Scouts of America in 1910.

186
00:17:11,640 --> 00:17:16,160
There are some that are 75, you know, 50, 40 years old.

187
00:17:16,400 --> 00:17:20,380
There are some that are 10 years old and there are some that don't last for more than five or 10 years.

188
00:17:20,380 --> 00:17:25,760
I'm not saying that what we're going to talk about is going to stop that kind of natural attrition over time.

189
00:17:25,900 --> 00:17:30,460
But if you're putting your time in and you believe in the program

190
00:17:30,460 --> 00:17:35,600
and you want to see it perpetuated, these are the questions that really need answering, aren't they?

191
00:17:35,780 --> 00:17:43,200
Many units are buoyed up by a strong leadership with expansive infectious vision

192
00:17:43,200 --> 00:17:45,800
that inspires a lot of interest and energy.

193
00:17:45,800 --> 00:17:50,300
They're hitting, you know, on all eight cylinders of the program and things are going great.

194
00:17:50,380 --> 00:17:56,840
And usually you can look at that unit and you're going to see one, two, three charismatic leaders

195
00:17:56,840 --> 00:18:00,640
who are usually the source of all the inspiration.

196
00:18:01,660 --> 00:18:07,620
Now, when those one, two or three leaders leave, then the inspiration can leave with them.

197
00:18:08,380 --> 00:18:10,840
So if you're in a key leadership position right now,

198
00:18:10,840 --> 00:18:19,179
you owe it not only to yourself, but you owe it to the next, you know, 150 boys

199
00:18:19,179 --> 00:18:24,800
who are going to be a part of that program to begin thinking five years out, 10 years out,

200
00:18:24,800 --> 00:18:31,060
and begin thinking about how you're going to make the transitions of leadership work

201
00:18:31,060 --> 00:18:35,500
and how you're going to help maintain the program when you're not there,

202
00:18:35,500 --> 00:18:40,440
when you're, when you've gone on and moved on to other things.

203
00:18:41,560 --> 00:18:48,060
Now, there's a lot of things that contribute to the success and relevance of scouting in a particular place and time.

204
00:18:48,300 --> 00:18:52,880
There's, you know, demographics and other various influences.

205
00:18:52,880 --> 00:19:03,000
But we set all that aside and we think, what are the big, broad concepts that help a scout unit stay alive over time

206
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,720
and maintain its service to the community?

207
00:19:05,720 --> 00:19:08,180
Because that's the aim of the whole thing, isn't it?

208
00:19:08,860 --> 00:19:12,480
Over the next four podcasts, we're going to talk about these things.

209
00:19:12,480 --> 00:19:18,020
We're going to see if we can paint some broad concepts that you can think about

210
00:19:18,020 --> 00:19:21,040
and apply to the situation you find yourself in.

211
00:19:21,260 --> 00:19:24,040
And the next time, what we're going to do is we're going to talk about membership

212
00:19:24,040 --> 00:19:28,400
and how many youth and adult members do you really need to stay alive

213
00:19:28,400 --> 00:19:31,480
and to keep your program moving forward.

214
00:19:42,480 --> 00:20:05,720
Well, thank you for listening to another edition of the Scoutmaster podcast.

215
00:20:06,080 --> 00:20:08,600
This has been podcast number 26.

216
00:20:08,600 --> 00:20:14,340
You can read the Scoutmaster blog at scoutmaster.typepad.com

217
00:20:14,340 --> 00:20:18,540
and you can follow us at Scoutmaster blog on Facebook

218
00:20:18,540 --> 00:20:22,800
and ScoutmasterCG on Twitter.

